{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"function-pointers-std-function","title":"Function Pointers, Functors & std::function","group":"Functions","order":17,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Three ways to treat “a function” as a value: raw function pointers (C-style), function objects (classes with <code>operator()</code>), and the type-erased <code>std::function</code> wrapper.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pointer\"></i>Function pointers</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }\nint mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }\n\nint apply(int (*op)(int, int), int x, int y) { return op(x, y); }\n\nusing BinaryOp = int (*)(int, int);          // alias makes the syntax readable\n\nint main() {\n    BinaryOp ops[] = {add, mul};             // table of functions\n    for (BinaryOp op : ops) std::cout &lt;&lt; apply(op, 3, 4) &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    int (*p)(int, int) = &amp;add;               // &amp; and * are optional for functions\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; (*p)(1, 1) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; p(2, 2) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7 12\n2 4</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Functors (function objects)</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nclass InRange {                               // a functor can carry state\n    int lo_, hi_;\npublic:\n    InRange(int lo, int hi) : lo_(lo), hi_(hi) {}\n    bool operator()(int x) const { return lo_ &lt;= x &amp;&amp; x &lt;= hi_; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 5, 9, 12, 7};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), InRange(5, 10)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), std::greater&lt;int&gt;());   // standard functor from &lt;functional&gt;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v.front() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3\n12</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plug-connected\"></i><code>std::function</code></h3>\n<p><code>std::function&lt;R(Args...)&gt;</code> can hold <em>any</em> callable with that signature — function pointer, functor or lambda (even with captures). It's the tool for storing callbacks of unknown type, at the cost of a possible heap allocation and an indirect call.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;map&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nint sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\n\nint main() {\n    int bonus = 100;\n    std::map&lt;std::string, std::function&lt;int(int, int)&gt;&gt; calc{\n        {\"+\", [](int a, int b) { return a + b; }},\n        {\"-\", sub},\n        {\"max\", [](int a, int b) { return std::max(a, b); }},\n        {\"bonus\", [bonus](int a, int) { return a + bonus; }},\n    };\n    for (const auto&amp; [name, fn] : calc) std::cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; fn(8, 3) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::function&lt;void()&gt; empty;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::boolalpha &lt;&lt; static_cast&lt;bool&gt;(empty) &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // calling it would throw bad_function_call\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>+ 11\n- 5\nbonus 108\nmax 8\nfalse</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i><code>std::bind</code> and <code>std::invoke</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;functional&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nint power(int base, int exp) { int r = 1; while (exp--) r *= base; return r; }\nstruct Account { int balance = 50; int get() const { return balance; } };\n\nint main() {\n    using namespace std::placeholders;\n    auto square = std::bind(power, _1, 2);           // fix the second argument\n    auto squareL = [](int b) { return power(b, 2); }; // the lambda equivalent (preferred)\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; square(7) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; squareL(7) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    Account acc;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; std::invoke(&amp;Account::get, acc) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::invoke(power, 2, 5) &lt;&lt; '\\n';  // uniform call syntax\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>49 49\n50 32</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Overhead</th><th>Holds state?</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Function pointer</td><td>Indirect call</td><td>No</td><td>C APIs, simple tables of functions</td></tr><tr><td>Lambda / functor + template parameter</td><td><strong>None</strong> (inlined)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Algorithms, hot paths: <code>template &lt;class F&gt; void run(F f)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>std::function</code></td><td>Type erasure; maybe allocation</td><td>Yes</td><td>Storing heterogeneous callbacks (maps, members, event handlers)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is std::sort with a lambda often faster than C's qsort?</summary><div><p><code>qsort</code> calls the comparator through a <code>void*</code>-based function pointer on every comparison, which the compiler usually can't inline. <code>std::sort</code> is a template: the lambda's type is part of the instantiation, so the comparison is inlined into the sorting loop.</p></div></details></section>"}